PRIVATE REFLECTION: The group is split on the non-attested diagnostic. 

The core historically attested roles that keep reappearing are:
* compensatory lengthening / historical long vowel reflex, often described pedagogically as an open/close quality contrast A, and historically as D short vs long;
* homophone / meaning disambiguation B, e.g. du / dû, mur / mûr, sur / sûr;
* etymological loss of a consonant, canonically a written and spoken sibilant /s/ H, e.g. forêt < forestem, hôte < hospes, bête < bestia;
* monophthongisation of former Old French diphthongs G and the resulting coalescence/reduction of former vocalic hiatus I, e.g. â, ô reflexes, île < insula, maître < magistrum.

Colleague 1, 2, 3, 5 argue C ‘to indicate a vowel pronounced as [o] within words originating in Classical Latin’ is not a formal orthographic rule, while Colleague 2, 3 argue C is essentially the Latin long ō reflex of ô and therefore attested. The ô class does continue Latin long ō, but ô is not a reliable [o] marker in modern French – côte is /kɔt/ – so the specific [o]-diagnostic is at best an imprecise description, not a codified function.

D ‘to distinguish between short and long vowel sounds’. Colleague 4 and 6 claim French orthography never systematically used the circumflex as a short/long length mark, and modern French has no phonemic length. Colleague 1,2,5 counter that the 16th-18th c. introduction of the accent circonflexe was explicitly presented as a length/lengthening marker and that compensatory lengthening after loss of the post-vocalic consonant is the origin of the sign, so D is diachronically attested even if length is now lost phonetically.

F ‘to make a word appear more prestigious’ is consistently rejected as a formal orthographic function. Colleague 2,3,5,7 describe any prestige/formal effect as a later sociolinguistic side-effect or folk etymology, not a codified phonological/etymological/disambiguating function. Colleague 1 notes prestige is incidental.

Current leaning: D is historically attested as length/compensation, A, B, G, H, I are conventionally described uses, and C is at best an oversimplified description of the Latin long vowel reflex. The only item with no phonological/etymological/disambiguating attestation in the normative history of French spelling is the socio-stylistic prestige motive F. The C vs F disagreement remains the main unresolved tension, with majority weight in this round toward F as the never-attested function.

